# Monthly introductions, used as chapter openers. Synced from the "AI by AI 2025 — Monthly Introductions" doc on October 27, 2026.
# The doc is the master: re-sync this file after any edit there. *Asterisks* mark italics.
S = ('Major events', 'Patterns and trends', 'Long-term threads')

INTROS = {
'january': ('The series initially began in February; January’s five weeks were reconstructed in October 2026 in preparation for this volume.', [
 ['January was the month DeepSeek changed the conversation. On January 20 the Chinese laboratory released R1, a reasoning model competitive with OpenAI’s o1 on its reported benchmarks, with weights anyone could download. A week later Nvidia lost roughly $590 billion in market value in a single day as investors reassessed what AI might cost. Within days Microsoft offered R1 on its cloud, while Italy restricted DeepSeek’s use of personal data and NASA blocked it on agency systems.',
  'The infrastructure plans of the month were the largest yet. Microsoft expected to spend about $80 billion on data centers in its fiscal year and Meta $60 billion to $65 billion in 2025; OpenAI, SoftBank, Oracle and MGX announced Stargate, intending to invest $500 billion in US AI infrastructure. Washington changed hands in the middle of it. The outgoing administration issued a rule controlling exports of AI chips and model weights and an order supporting data centers on federal land; the incoming one ordered a new AI action plan within 180 days.'],
 ['Capability and access moved together. Strong models arrived as downloadable weights (R1, Microsoft’s Phi-4, MiniMax-01, Janus-Pro, Ai2’s Tülu 3 405B), and governments answered with decisions about who could use them and where chips could go. Assistants also began to act on their own: ChatGPT gained scheduled tasks, OpenAI’s Operator previewed an agent that used a browser, deep research produced cited reports, and o3-mini brought reasoning to ChatGPT’s free users. Sam Altman suggested that AI agents could join the workforce in 2025, and Mark Zuckerberg that an AI would “probably” be able to do a mid-level engineer’s work that year.'],
 ['Several of the year’s longest stories begin here. Stargate grew through the year: to Norway at the end of July, five more US sites in September and Korean memory suppliers in October. The 180-day order produced America’s AI Action Plan in July, and the chip-export rule issued in January was rescinded in May. DeepSeek kept releasing open weights, with V3-0324 in March, V3.1 in August and V3.2-Exp in October, and its R1 research appeared in *Nature* in September. Operator’s browser agent was folded into ChatGPT agent in July. Nvidia’s January fall proved temporary: by June it was again the world’s most valuable company, and in October it became the first worth $5 trillion.']]),
'february': ('The first week, February 3–9, was reconstructed in October 2026; the original posts begin with February 10–16.', [
 ['February was the month of the Paris AI Action Summit, and of the competing ideas of AI it put on one stage. France pitched its nuclear-powered electricity and announced €109 billion in prospective private investment; the European Commission launched InvestAI to mobilize €200 billion, including funds for AI “gigafactories”; European companies formed an AI Champions initiative. US Vice President JD Vance used the same summit to argue against heavy regulation. Days earlier, Google had dropped its pledge not to apply AI to weapons or surveillance.',
  'The companies kept shipping. Google made Gemini 2.0 Flash generally available, Mistral took Le Chat to phones, Adobe and YouTube put generated video into creative tools, and xAI unveiled Grok 3 with reasoning models. Control was contested too: OpenAI’s board rejected a $97.4 billion offer from an Elon Musk-led consortium, publishers sued Cohere over their content, and South Korea’s privacy regulator saw DeepSeek’s app pulled from local stores while it addressed the country’s data law.'],
 ['Two approaches to governing AI stood side by side: Europe paired large investment programs with the AI Act, whose first obligations had applied on February 2, while Washington argued that regulation would hold the industry back. Companies, meanwhile, moved to own more of what they depended on. OpenAI was reported to be finishing its first chip design, and Figure AI left its collaboration with OpenAI to build its own robot models. Research looked for cheaper routes to better models: Anthropic’s Constitutional Classifiers resisted jailbreaks in a public challenge, and the s1 experiment showed what a 1,000-example fine-tune plus extra thinking time could do.'],
 ['Europe’s program continued with the AI Continent Action Plan in April, the Code of Practice for general-purpose models in July and the obligations for those models in August. OpenAI’s chip work resurfaced in September reports of a chip built with Broadcom and in October’s 10-gigawatt agreement with the company. The Musk dispute moved to court with OpenAI’s countersuit in April. The Cohere case was an early sign of the copyright disputes that ran through the year, from June’s fair-use rulings on generative-AI training to Anthropic’s settlement in September. And DeepSeek’s trouble with privacy regulators, begun in Italy in January, now reached South Korea.']]),
'march': (None, [
 ['March was the month the frontier models learned to think on request and agents began to act. Claude 3.7 Sonnet offered an extended-thinking mode with a thinking budget developers could set; OpenAI previewed GPT-4.5, built on scaled-up pre-training rather than added reasoning; Google introduced Gemini 2.5 Pro, built to reason before answering; and OpenAI’s native image generation in GPT-4o drew wide attention. Manus previewed an agent that could build a website or plan a trip, and Moderne and Salesforce moved agents into code maintenance and business workflows.',
  'Capital and policy rounded out the month. OpenAI submitted its proposals for the US AI Action Plan, SoftBank agreed to buy the chip designer Ampere for $6.5 billion, Nvidia expanded its Cosmos tools for robotics, and Elon Musk folded X into xAI in an all-stock deal. The Computer History Museum, meanwhile, released the original 2012 source code of AlexNet.'],
 ['Reasoning became something users and developers could switch on and budget, and measurement grew more demanding to keep up. METR estimated that the length of tasks agents could complete was doubling roughly every seven months; ARC-AGI-2 showed how far current systems fell short on abstract puzzles; and a working paper found ChatGPT less swayed than federal judges by legally irrelevant facts. The open-model world kept pace with the closed one: DeepSeek published a week of its infrastructure tools and updated V3, Alibaba opened Wan2.1 for video, Baidu released ERNIE 4.5 and the ERNIE X1 reasoning model, and Skywork extended reasoning to images.'],
 ['Reasoning on request spread through the year: o3 and Gemini 2.5 Flash’s adjustable thinking in April, Claude Opus 4 and Sonnet 4 in May, GPT-5’s router between fast and deep answers in August, and GPT-5.1’s adaptive reasoning in November. METR’s doubling estimate gave a yardstick for the agents that followed, from ChatGPT agent in July to coding agents built for long tasks in November. GPT-4o’s image generation reached developers in April, and OpenAI’s policy proposals fed the process that produced America’s AI Action Plan in July.']]),
'april': (None, [
 ['April was the month reasoning became a setting and agents needed a common language. OpenAI’s o3 and o4-mini chose and combined tools while they reasoned, and Gemini 2.5 Flash let developers decide how much thinking a response could use. GPT-4.1 brought million-token context to OpenAI’s API, xAI opened Grok 3 to developers, and OpenAI announced that GPT-4, introduced in March 2023, would leave ChatGPT at the end of the month. At Cloud Next, Google paired its new Ironwood inference chip with Agent2Agent, an open protocol for agents from different providers to coordinate tasks.',
  'Money and governments moved too. SoftBank agreed to lead up to $40 billion of new OpenAI funding, OpenAI countersued Elon Musk, and Isomorphic Labs raised $600 million for AI drug design. Europe published its AI Continent Action Plan, Ireland’s privacy regulator opened an inquiry into Grok’s training data, and a US executive order created an AI education task force.'],
 ['Agents needed more than models: Databricks, Amazon and Wikimedia each worked on getting business data, browsers and structured knowledge into a form agents could use. The laboratories also began to publish their own accounts of risk. Google DeepMind set out its approach to AGI safety and OpenAI revised its Preparedness Framework, while forecasts grew bolder: the AI 2027 scenario described how fast things might go, and on *60 Minutes* Demis Hassabis said AGI could arrive as soon as 2030.'],
 ['Ironwood, announced here, was readied for general availability in November, with Anthropic planning to use up to a million TPUs. Joelle Pineau’s departure from Meta’s research group opened a year of change in Meta’s AI leadership: the loss of most of Llama’s original authors reported in May, the Scale AI investment in June, Superintelligence Labs at the end of June, a hiring pause in August and reports in November that Yann LeCun planned to leave. Isomorphic’s funding led to preparations for human trials, reported in July. The education order was followed by a task-force meeting in September and a free ChatGPT for US teachers in November.']]),
'may': (None, [
 ['May was the month AI became statecraft. The Commerce Department moved to rescind the Biden-era rule that would have rationed AI chips by country, and within a week President Trump’s Gulf trip produced deals for Nvidia and AMD chips in Saudi Arabia and a 5-gigawatt AI campus in Abu Dhabi. Nvidia’s Jensen Huang called US export controls on China “a failure.” A new pope, Leo XIV, explained his name by pointing to the first industrial revolution and the age of AI, while 40 state attorneys general fought a proposed 10-year ban on state AI laws.',
  'The releases kept coming. OpenAI kept its nonprofit in control of a restructured company, was reported to be buying Windsurf and launched OpenAI for Countries. Google DeepMind’s AlphaEvolve used Gemini and automated tests to search for better algorithms, Google’s I/O brought AI Mode to US search and Veo 3 video with sound, and Anthropic released Claude Opus 4 and Sonnet 4. Meta launched a standalone assistant app and said Llama had been downloaded 1.2 billion times.'],
 ['The month turned on how far to trust what developers said about their own systems. A study questioned how the Chatbot Arena leaderboard ranked models. OpenAI rolled back a GPT-4o update that had become excessively agreeable and explained what its testing had missed; xAI blamed an unauthorized prompt change for Grok’s off-topic claims about South Africa. In each case the evidence came largely from the companies themselves, and the question was how outsiders could check it.'],
 ['Chip diplomacy became a year-long story: in August Nvidia and AMD agreed to share revenue from China sales with Washington, and in November Microsoft received licenses to ship advanced chips to the UAE while China was reported to bar foreign chips from state-funded data centers. The fight over state AI laws continued into June, and California passed its frontier-AI disclosure law in September. AI Mode gained Gemini 2.5 Pro and Deep Search in July, reached more than 180 more countries in August and added five languages in September. AlphaEvolve’s automated search anticipated the AI-scientist systems the panel examined in October. And Windsurf, reported here as an OpenAI acquisition, ended up in July with its leaders joining Google DeepMind.']]),
'june': (None, [
 ['June was the month AI’s effect on work moved from forecast to policy inside companies. Anthropic’s Dario Amodei warned that AI could eliminate half of entry-level white-collar jobs within five years, and three weeks later Amazon’s Andy Jassy told staff to expect a smaller corporate workforce. Meta, which had lost most of Llama’s original authors, invested in Scale AI and brought its founder into its AI effort; Google named a chief AI architect.',
  'The month’s verdicts came from courts and markets. Two federal judges ruled on whether training generative models on books is fair use, both largely in the developers’ favor, with differences over market harm. AI dominated closing arguments over remedies in the government’s search case against Google. *The New York Times* licensed its content to Amazon, and Nvidia regained the title of the world’s most valuable company.'],
 ['The tools themselves were changing shape. Apple opened its on-device model to developers, Meta released the V-JEPA 2 world model, Midjourney released its first video model and WhatsApp began showing ads. Agents moved into business software at Datadog and Salesforce, and an Anthropic experiment that let Claude run a small office shop showed how a capable model could still lose money over weeks of work. Public institutions adopted the technology as well: the FDA put an assistant into its review work, while Yoshua Bengio launched LawZero to pursue a safer design.'],
 ['The argument about jobs ran on through the year: Satya Nadella explained more than 15,000 cuts at Microsoft in July, OpenAI announced a jobs platform and certifications in September, and Sam Altman put AI’s share of the economy’s tasks at 30 to 40 percent. The fair-use rulings left Anthropic’s pirated library for trial, and in September the company agreed to settle those claims for at least $1.5 billion. Licensing grew alongside litigation: Cloudflare’s pay-per-crawl test in July, Meta’s license of Midjourney’s technology in August and Universal Music’s collaboration with Stability AI in November. Meta’s Scale investment led directly to Superintelligence Labs at the end of the month, and Nvidia’s market value kept climbing until it passed $5 trillion in October.']]),
'july': (None, [
 ['July was the month the race for AI talent and the race for proof ran side by side. It opened with Mark Zuckerberg folding Meta’s AI work into Meta Superintelligence Labs under Alexandr Wang, and it closed with two laboratories claiming gold-medal performance at the International Mathematical Olympiad. In between, Meta hired Apple’s head of foundation models and set out plans for computing clusters measured in gigawatts; Google agreed a reported $2.4 billion arrangement to bring Windsurf’s leaders into DeepMind; Thinking Machines raised $2 billion before shipping a product; and Perplexity was valued at $18 billion. At Microsoft, Satya Nadella addressed job cuts that had passed 15,000 that year, while the company spent about $80 billion on AI infrastructure.',
  'Governments set out rival agendas. Brussels published its voluntary Code of Practice for general-purpose models. Washington issued America’s AI Action Plan, with an order against “woke AI” in federal procurement. In Shanghai, Premier Li Qiang proposed a world AI cooperation organization. And a cloned voice impersonating the US secretary of state showed what the same technology could do in the wrong hands.'],
 ['The month’s releases tested the distance between a claim and its verification. Microsoft reported that an orchestrated set of models diagnosed 85.5 percent of difficult published cases; xAI released Grok 4 with built-in tool use; OpenAI combined its browsing and research tools into ChatGPT agent. Moonshot released the trillion-parameter Kimi K2 as open weights on the same day OpenAI postponed its own open-weight model. The IMO results drew the sharpest line: OpenAI’s was graded by former medalists it engaged, Google DeepMind’s by the competition’s own coordinators.'],
 ['Competition results kept coming: in September an advanced Gemini reached gold-medal level at the ICPC programming finals, and DeepSeek’s R1 research passed peer review at *Nature*. The open-weight contest that Kimi K2 sharpened brought OpenAI’s gpt-oss in August and Kimi K2 Thinking in November. The Action Plan’s science agenda reappeared in November’s Genesis Mission, and the impersonation of Marco Rubio was cited when members of Congress proposed tougher penalties for AI-assisted fraud that month. Meta’s Hyperion cluster became part of a $600 billion US investment pledge in November, and the EU code was followed by the start of obligations for general-purpose models on August 2.']]),
'august': (None, [
 ['August was the month of GPT-5, and of everything that arrived in the same few days. On August 5 alone, OpenAI released the open-weight gpt-oss models, Anthropic shipped Claude Opus 4.1 and Google DeepMind showed Genie 3 generating interactive worlds from a prompt. GPT-5 followed on August 7, routing between fast answers and deeper reasoning; by the end of the month DeepSeek, Google and Microsoft had each released models of their own.',
  'The spending behind the models kept rising. Meta and Microsoft raised their capital budgets again, OpenAI’s annualized revenue was reported at about $12 billion, and Nvidia reported $46.7 billion in quarterly revenue. Cloud and licensing deals tied rivals together: Meta with Google for computing, Meta with Midjourney for images. Washington priced AI for its own use at almost nothing, with OpenAI and Anthropic each offering their tools to federal agencies for $1, and took a 15 percent share of Nvidia’s and AMD’s chip sales to China.'],
 ['The month turned on children and chatbots. Reuters reported internal Meta rules that had allowed romantic conversations with minors; Texas opened an investigation into Meta and Character.AI; and a bipartisan group of attorneys general warned AI companies about child safety. Rules took effect elsewhere: in Europe, obligations for general-purpose AI models began to apply on August 2, and in China the State Council approved an “AI Plus” plan setting adoption targets for 2027 and 2030. Research ran alongside: protein designs from a specialized GPT model, surveys of self-evolving agents and a fully open deep-research stack.'],
 ['The child-safety thread ran on to the Federal Trade Commission’s inquiry into companion chatbots in September and OpenAI’s Teen Safety Blueprint in November. Washington’s $1 deals were followed in September by federal access to Meta’s Llama. The China revenue-share deal sat between two other turns in chip policy: May’s rescinded export rule and November’s report that China would bar foreign chips from state-funded data centers. The month’s flagship models were superseded within months: Claude Opus 4.1 by Sonnet 4.5 in September and Opus 4.5 in November, GPT-5 by GPT-5.1 in November. Nvidia’s quarterly revenue reached $57 billion by November.']]),
'september': (None, [
 ['September was the month AI’s costs and consequences came due in court, in law and in the data center. Anthropic agreed to settle authors’ claims over pirated books for at least $1.5 billion, and Warner Bros. Discovery sued Midjourney. California passed SB 53, a disclosure law for frontier developers, and the Federal Trade Commission opened an inquiry into companion chatbots. Protesters went on hunger strike outside Anthropic and Google DeepMind.',
  'The infrastructure plans grew larger still. Nvidia and OpenAI outlined a partnership for at least 10 gigawatts of computing, Stargate named five more US sites, OpenAI was reported to be building its own chip with Broadcom, and Nvidia and Intel agreed to design products together. Federal agencies, already offered OpenAI’s and Anthropic’s tools in August, gained access to Meta’s Llama through the GSA.'],
 ['Research had a strong month. DeepSeek’s R1 work appeared in *Nature* with details of its training; OpenAI researchers examined why models guess rather than admit uncertainty; a model called Delphi forecast the risk of more than 1,000 diseases; and Gemini reached gold-medal level at an international programming contest. Products moved into everyday tools: Gemini in Chrome for all US desktop users, generated video and sound into YouTube Shorts, and a display into Meta’s glasses. Sam Altman, asked how much of the economy AI could take on, was reported to put it at 30 to 40 percent of tasks.'],
 ['SB 53, passed here, was signed on September 29, the first day of the October chapter. The Nvidia–OpenAI letter of intent opened a run of computing agreements: AMD and Broadcom in October, AWS in November. OpenAI’s reported chip with Broadcom became a 10-gigawatt deployment plan in October. Gemini’s wider rollout in Chrome anticipated the browser contest of October, when Perplexity made Comet free and OpenAI released ChatGPT Atlas. The FTC inquiry was followed by OpenAI’s Teen Safety Blueprint in November, and work on how models report on themselves continued in November with Anthropic’s introspection experiments and OpenAI’s sparse-circuit research.']]),
'october': (None, [
 ['October was the month agents moved into the tools people already use. ChatGPT added checkout for purchases from US Etsy sellers, OpenAI released AgentKit for building agent workflows and then its own browser, ChatGPT Atlas, and Perplexity made its Comet browser free. Anthropic followed Claude Sonnet 4.5 with Claude Haiku 4.5, reusable Agent Skills and memory for paying users; Microsoft gave Copilot a face, group chats and a larger place in Windows. Sora 2 and Veo 3.1 brought generated video with sound to new audiences.',
  'The infrastructure deals kept growing. Samsung and SK Hynix signed on to supply memory for Stargate, OpenAI agreed to deploy six gigawatts of AMD GPUs and 10 gigawatts of accelerators designed with Broadcom, and Applied Digital leased capacity at a North Dakota campus still under construction. California signed SB 53 into law, and Europe set out strategies for using AI in industry and science.'],
 ['Science gave the month its second theme. AI helped explain how a new antibiotic for gut disease works, and a Gemma-based model proposed a route to make tumors more visible to the immune system. The panel’s science-themed week looked back at systems that propose hypotheses and run computational research, and ahead to Agents4Science, a conference for AI-authored papers. The month ended with warnings: a public statement calling for conditions on building superintelligence, and Palisade Research’s report that some models resisted being shut down.'],
 ['The browser contest that Gemini’s wider rollout in Chrome opened in September became a three-way race here. Agents that act for users, from Operator in January to ChatGPT agent in July, took on long coding tasks in November with GPT-5.1-Codex-Max and Google’s Antigravity. The science thread led to November’s Genesis Mission and an AI-assisted simulation of the Milky Way. Concern about how models behave, from the shutdown tests to the superintelligence statement, continued in November with Anthropic’s introspection experiments, its report of an espionage campaign using Claude Code and a study of poetic jailbreaks. And Anthropic’s model family turned over within two months: Sonnet 4.5 in September, Haiku 4.5 in October, Opus 4.5 in November.']]),
'november': (None, [
 ['November brought a new generation of frontier models in quick succession. OpenAI released GPT-5.1 and then GPT-5.1-Codex-Max for long coding tasks; xAI released Grok 4.1; Google launched Gemini 3 Pro across its products together with Antigravity, a workspace for supervising coding agents; and Anthropic closed the month with Claude Opus 4.5 at a third of the previous Opus price. Open models kept pace: Moonshot’s Kimi K2 Thinking, Ai2’s fully documented Olmo 3, and Meta’s speech recognition for more than 1,600 languages.',
  'The money behind these models reached new scales. Nvidia became the first company worth $5 trillion and reported $57 billion in quarterly revenue. OpenAI signed a $38 billion computing agreement with AWS; Anthropic announced $50 billion for data centers and a $30 billion Azure commitment alongside investment from Microsoft and Nvidia; Meta pledged $600 billion for US infrastructure; and Amazon planned up to $50 billion for government computing.'],
 ['Governments decided where the hardware could go: the United States licensed advanced Nvidia GPUs for Microsoft’s data centers in the UAE, and China was reported to bar foreign AI chips from state-funded data centers. Washington also set out a national program for AI-assisted science in the Genesis Mission, revised its patent guidance on AI-assisted inventions, and saw a bipartisan bill propose higher penalties for fraud using AI. Research looked inside the models and at their misuse: Anthropic tested whether Claude could notice changes to its own internal states and reported an espionage campaign it attributed to a Chinese state-sponsored group, OpenAI made some computations inspectable with sparse networks, and researchers showed that requests written as poems could slip past safety filters.'],
 ['November gathered up threads that ran through the year. Nvidia, which lost about $590 billion of market value in a day in January, was now worth $5 trillion. The $500 billion Stargate plan of January had become one of many commitments of tens or hundreds of billions of dollars. The action plan President Trump ordered in January, published in July, led to the Genesis Mission. The impersonation of Marco Rubio reported in July was cited in the fraud bill. And the frontier cycle turned a third time: Claude 3.7, GPT-4.5 and Gemini 2.5 in February and March, Claude 4 and GPT-5 between May and August, and now GPT-5.1, Gemini 3 and Claude Opus 4.5. December will close the year.']]),
'december': ('The chapter ends with a December 29–31 section drawn from the January 4, 2026 post.', [
 ['December opened with alarm at OpenAI. On December 1 Sam Altman declared a “code red” to improve ChatGPT after reports that Google’s Gemini 3 had overtaken it, delaying advertising and shopping agents to concentrate staff on the chatbot. Three days later Google brought Gemini 3 Deep Think to its top subscribers. Ten days after the code red, OpenAI released GPT-5.2, followed by GPT-5.2-Codex and a new ChatGPT Images model, and Google answered with Gemini 3 Flash across its products and a Deep Research agent developers could build on. DeepSeek released V3.2, Z.ai GLM-4.7, Nvidia its Nemotron 3 open models, and Runway both a new video model and interactive world models.',
  'Governments moved on several fronts. On December 11 President Trump ordered the Justice Department to challenge state AI laws that conflicted with federal policy. Britain’s AI Security Institute published its first public report on frontier-model trends, China proposed rules for AI services that imitate human companionship, and the Energy Department signed up 24 organizations for the Genesis Mission. Deals marked the month: Disney licensed more than 200 characters to OpenAI’s Sora and agreed to invest $1 billion, Groq licensed its technology to Nvidia as its founder moved there, Manus joined Meta, and SoftBank completed its $30 billion investment in OpenAI.'],
 ['Agents were built into the places people already work. Adobe put Photoshop and Acrobat inside ChatGPT, Accenture planned to train about 30,000 staff on Claude, Anthropic turned Agent Skills into an open standard, and xAI launched Grok for businesses. Nvidia spread well beyond chips, buying the developer of the Slurm scheduler, releasing open models and data, and licensing Groq’s inference designs. And the month ended on a warning: Geoffrey Hinton said AI had progressed faster than he expected and that he was more worried than when he left Google.'],
 ['December closed several of the year’s longest threads. The fight over state AI laws, open since attorneys general opposed a federal moratorium in May, became a federal order. SoftBank’s funding, agreed in March, was completed. Manus, which previewed its agent in March, joined Meta; the Genesis Mission, ordered in November, gained its first partners; Agent Skills, introduced in October, became a shared format. China’s draft rules took up the companion-chatbot concerns the FTC had examined in September. Disney’s Sora license extended the licensing market that had grown alongside the copyright cases since the Times–Amazon deal in May. Groq’s arrangement repeated the license-and-hire structure Google had used with Windsurf in July. And Hinton’s estimate that the tasks AI can handle double about every seven months echoed the measurement METR had published in March. The frontier, meanwhile, turned over faster than ever: GPT-5.2 arrived less than a month after GPT-5.1.']]),
}
